      "id": "fb-001",
      "question": "What happens when a SIM goes offline?",
      "shape": "document",
      "guard": "fix",
      "blocked_on": null,
      "asked_as": "1a1db65e-52d5-493b-a1a9-7e495005530a",
      "http_status": 200,
      "seconds": 28.35,
      "answer": "When a SIM goes offline, several user stories describe how the system is designed to handle it:\n\n1. **Visibility and Troubleshooting**: Each SIM is visibly linked to a specific tracker, asset, branch, and department to avoid confusion during support calls and speed up troubleshooting.\n\n2. **Data Spend Monitoring**: Finance managers receive data-spend summaries by telco, country, product, and customer account to prevent unnoticed margin erosion.\n\n3. **Automatic SIM Data Ingestion**: The system is designed to automatically ingest SIM data from telco APIs where possible, improving accuracy and operating efficiency.\n\n4. **SIM Commissioning**: Guided steps capture necessary details like ICCID, IMEI, APN, test signal, and first heartbeat to ensure correct installation and reduce repeat visits.\n\n5. **Roaming Status and Cost Impact**: Cross-border fleet owners can see roaming status and estimated cost impact as soon as a unit crosses a border to avoid shock bills.\n\n6. **Bundle Recommendations**: The system recommends suitable roaming or local bundles for the corridor an asset is entering to keep units online at a lower cost.\n\n7. **Bundle Monitoring**: Support engineers can see bundle remaining percentages and projected depletion dates for active SIMs to intervene before a unit goes offline.\n\n8. **Connectivity Gaps**: Wearable and smartphone SIMs show network type, signal strength, and recent connectivity gaps to determine if staff are truly unreachable.\n\nThese are part of the documented design and user stories, not a confirmation of what is live on your account.\n\nSource: OLIWA User Stories — this is the documented design; I can't confirm from here which parts are live.",
      "tools_used": [
